    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The above observed decreasing trends of compressive properties with increasing mesostructural stochastics can be explained through the weakest link principle (see also [ 61 , 62 ]). Introducing the stochastic variations of more mesostructural features gives rise to the emergence of more weak regions. Indeed, Figure 19 demonstrates that the cell wall buckling events (using eq. ( 36 )) tend to get promoted as more mesostructural stochastics are included (from “CtCt” to “StSt”). Here, N c and N w denote the number of buckled cell walls and the total number of cell walls, respectively. The fractions N c /N w of buckled cell walls for different model sets eventually become comparable upon a large applied strain. As expected, the cell wall buckling events for H200 are postponed (see also Figure 17 ) and accompanied by a slower growth of N c /N w , compared with H100."
